“They say it’s humility,” they whisper. “We’re saving your soul.” Sure. Let me be clear: humility is wearing a collar while knowing you’re not a dog. I am not a dog; I’m an opinion, a spark, a human being who has been turned into a living trophy. Every night the entire town—yes, the whole mob—steps into my room like a parade of judgmental leashes, eager to make sure my every breath is a spectacle.

You see those smug faces? The same ones that welcomed me into this twisted zoo. In their eyes, I’m nothing but an object. No longer am I a person; I’ve become a prop, a toy to be tugged and laughed at. And the dogs? They bark only at me. They ignore everyone else as if they’re on my side, creating a world where all you can hear is your own howl. That’s what I’ve become in Dogville: a loud, lonely cry that slices through their applause.

The cold, clear rage inside me isn’t for them; it’s for everyone who thinks it’s possible to tame a person without seeing how much they’re abandoning their humanity. They think this collar will teach me humility, but the only lesson I’ve learned is that you can’t silence what knows how to make itself heard.

